{"author":"Rebar Bot","author_email":"joeoakhart+bot@navapbc.com","author_id":"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d","author_sig":"{\"payload\": \"\", \"payloadType\": \"application/vnd.rebar.authorship.v1+json\", \"signatures\": [{\"keyid\": \"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d\", \"sig\": \"\"}]}","data":{"advisory":[{"agreement":1,"block_threshold":0.54,"blocking_enabled":true,"checklist_item":"- [ ] Make the native-separator journal test write a literal backslash (`f\"{tid}\\\\{snap.name}\"`) and make the probe convert it on every platform, so the test fails on Linux if the conversion is removed","criteria":["tests"],"decision":"advisory","evidence":["tests/unit/test_compact_crash_atomicity.py:524: `record[\"snapshot\"] = os.path.join(tid, snap.name)`: on POSIX this is `<tid>/<name>`, identical to what write_intent already stored","tests/unit/test_compact_crash_atomicity.py:514-515 docstring claims it covers ``<tid>\\x-SNAPSHOT.json``, but no backslash is ever written on POSIX","src/rebar/_commands/compact_recovery.py:98: `probe = _git(tracker, \"cat-file\", \"-e\", f\"HEAD:{PurePath(rel_snapshot).as_posix()}\")`: on POSIX, PurePath is PurePosixPath, so the conversion is a no-op there","Named violating input: on Linux, reverting line 98 to `f\"HEAD:{rel_snapshot}\"` still passes this test, because the journal holds `<tid>/<name>`","Similarly, test_crash_state_is_json_round_trippable (test_compact_crash_atomicity.py:543) asserts `f\"{tid}/x-SNAPSHOT.json\"`, which os.path.relpath already produced on POSIX, so the write-side as_posix() change is also only checked on Windows",".github/workflows/test.yml:262-264: sweep-windows runs only on `schedule`/`workflow_dispatch` with `continue-on-error: true`"],"finding":"test_a_native_separator_journal_still_finds_its_committed_snapshot builds its 'native-separator' journal with os.path.join. On Linux and macOS (the platforms of the merge-blocking CI) that already yields the forward-slash form the fix writes, so the test never feeds `_snapshot_in_head` a 